WEBVTT

00:00:01.650 --> 00:00:02.640
INBAL SAGIV: Hi, everyone.

00:00:02.640 --> 00:00:06.240
My name is Inbal Sagiv, and
I'm Principal Product Manager

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:11.760
at Microsoft, working mainly
on AI that runs locally.

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:15.630
The session today is going to
focus on extending Foundry Local

00:00:15.630 --> 00:00:18.720
for enterprise and
disconnected environments.

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:21.840
I'm going to walk you through
some context from the market,

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:25.230
then I'll speak through the AI
offering, the model offering,

00:00:25.230 --> 00:00:30.100
the agent framework, and we'll
close with some video agents.

00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:33.810
Let me start with
the big picture.

00:00:33.810 --> 00:00:38.670
We're in a once-in-a-generation
platform shift from applications

00:00:38.670 --> 00:00:43.050
to agentic AI as the
operating layer.

00:00:43.050 --> 00:00:45.420
The data, as you see in
that slide, is clear.

00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:51.030
We have 1.3 billion
AI agents by 2028,

00:00:51.030 --> 00:00:54.330
which are automating
end-to-end business processes.

00:00:54.330 --> 00:00:59.250
We see 82 percentages of
organizations adopting agents

00:00:59.250 --> 00:01:05.790
in the next three years, moving
from pilots to core workforce.

00:01:05.790 --> 00:01:09.600
And we see additional numbers
that coming from our analysts.

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:12.090
Bottom line, the takeaway
is pretty simple.

00:01:12.090 --> 00:01:15.300
The organizations
that operation --

00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:19.950
working today with some
AI agent now need to move

00:01:19.950 --> 00:01:22.530
and define how the next decade

00:01:22.530 --> 00:01:25.560
of software is going
to build and run.

00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:32.490
And everything is about agents
and agentic capabilities.

00:01:32.490 --> 00:01:36.900
Now, let's see some of the
scenarios that we hear

00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:40.080
from our customers because
we want to ground everything

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:43.560
that we ship with
customer use cases.

00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:46.860
These are not optional
design choices.

00:01:46.860 --> 00:01:51.540
These are hard constraints
geopolitical risks,

00:01:51.540 --> 00:01:55.920
regulatory control, the need
to operate through outages,

00:01:55.920 --> 00:01:58.140
the growing pressure to adopt AI

00:01:58.140 --> 00:02:00.660
without giving up
on sovereignty.

00:02:00.660 --> 00:02:04.140
The bottom line here
is that customer says

00:02:04.140 --> 00:02:09.390
that AI strategy has to work
when actually things are break.

00:02:09.390 --> 00:02:13.620
This is not just when everything
is running smoothly on cloud.

00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:16.980
Now you see here multiple type
of use cases and scenario,

00:02:16.980 --> 00:02:22.070
but let me just pick
two items here.

00:02:22.070 --> 00:02:26.760
One of these are actually
the public safety.

00:02:26.760 --> 00:02:30.210
When you are running a
command center or responding

00:02:30.210 --> 00:02:33.180
to a crisis, there is no
tolerance for latency,

00:02:33.180 --> 00:02:35.850
for dependency, or for outages.

00:02:35.850 --> 00:02:40.020
AI needs to be operated fully
disconnected while still

00:02:40.020 --> 00:02:43.680
delivering real-time situational
awareness and decision support.

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:48.090
No external calls, no data which
has been sent to the cloud.

00:02:48.090 --> 00:02:52.890
If the network goes down,
the system cannot goes down.

00:02:52.890 --> 00:02:55.740
We see it also from
critical infrastructures

00:02:55.740 --> 00:02:57.330
like energy and utilities.

00:02:57.330 --> 00:03:00.840
These environments are
distributed, often remote,

00:03:00.840 --> 00:03:05.790
and in these scenarios,
connectivity is not guaranteed.

00:03:05.790 --> 00:03:09.360
Yet, they rely on AI for
real-time monitoring,

00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:13.020
for diagnostics, and
incident responses.

00:03:13.020 --> 00:03:17.580
If a substation or a rail
system loses connectivity,

00:03:17.580 --> 00:03:19.680
operation does not.

00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:22.650
The AI must continue
running locally safety

00:03:22.650 --> 00:03:24.540
and with compliance.

00:03:24.540 --> 00:03:26.790
So when you step back,
this is the shift.

00:03:26.790 --> 00:03:30.360
AI is no longer just about
performance or scale,

00:03:30.360 --> 00:03:32.910
but it's actually about
resilience and control,

00:03:32.910 --> 00:03:37.140
and the ability to operate
under these constraints.

00:03:37.140 --> 00:03:41.730
The organizations solving
for these now are those ones

00:03:41.730 --> 00:03:43.200
that will actually be able

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:47.560
to deploy the AI
everywhere it matters.

00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:55.170
If we look at the portfolio that
Microsoft offers, when it comes

00:03:55.170 --> 00:03:58.800
to sovereign cloud, this allow
the customers the freedom

00:03:58.800 --> 00:04:00.720
to choose the right
balance of control,

00:04:00.720 --> 00:04:04.050
capability, and autonomy.

00:04:04.050 --> 00:04:05.760
Sovereign AI is about control,

00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:07.590
not just about the
location itself.

00:04:07.590 --> 00:04:10.800
So, in the middle, you see
"sovereign private cloud,"

00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:15.300
which is a cloud environment,
operated under full customer

00:04:15.300 --> 00:04:17.850
or national control,
where data and the models

00:04:17.850 --> 00:04:22.320
and the operations remain
within a defined regulatory

00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:24.240
or geographic boundaries.

00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:28.260
It combines the cloud
capabilities with an isolated

00:04:28.260 --> 00:04:31.590
and compliant
infrastructure, Azure Local,

00:04:31.590 --> 00:04:35.880
that can operate independently,
ensuring the continuity,

00:04:35.880 --> 00:04:38.070
the control, and
the resilience even

00:04:38.070 --> 00:04:42.930
without external cloud
connectivity, as I mentioned.

00:04:42.930 --> 00:04:47.130
Now, we're focusing on this
context, before I'll dive

00:04:47.130 --> 00:04:51.630
into the entire AI offering,
it's very important

00:04:51.630 --> 00:04:57.510
to understand what is Azure
Local and why we actually run

00:04:57.510 --> 00:05:01.140
on that Azure Local
infrastructure.

00:05:01.140 --> 00:05:04.470
So Azure Local is not new.

00:05:04.470 --> 00:05:07.950
We're actually GA on both
offering that is running

00:05:07.950 --> 00:05:10.500
on a connected and disconnected,

00:05:10.500 --> 00:05:14.010
fully disconnected
sovereign use cases.

00:05:14.010 --> 00:05:15.810
And this is actually
the foundation

00:05:15.810 --> 00:05:17.040
that makes the sovereign

00:05:17.040 --> 00:05:19.740
and enterprise AI
possible on premises.

00:05:19.740 --> 00:05:21.420
It's not just a server.

00:05:21.420 --> 00:05:27.210
It's a proposed built-in
AI-optimized infrastructure

00:05:27.210 --> 00:05:30.930
platform that designed to run
the full Foundry Local stack.

00:05:30.930 --> 00:05:33.210
I'll speak through
this in a minute.

00:05:33.210 --> 00:05:36.450
And when I refer to Foundry
Local, I really refer

00:05:36.450 --> 00:05:41.850
from models to inferencing to
agentic workflows entirely,

00:05:41.850 --> 00:05:45.720
where within the
customer-own environment.

00:05:45.720 --> 00:05:47.040
Look at the hardware layer.

00:05:47.040 --> 00:05:51.570
So Azure Local delivers AI
optimized hardware configuration

00:05:51.570 --> 00:05:54.750
that spanning across
CPU, NPU, and GPU.

00:05:54.750 --> 00:05:59.190
It's validated and
certified to run a set

00:05:59.190 --> 00:06:02.610
of Foundry workloads
out of the box.

00:06:02.610 --> 00:06:04.560
When you need a
single-node inferencing

00:06:04.560 --> 00:06:07.680
or a lightweight model or
a multi-node GPU cluster

00:06:07.680 --> 00:06:09.420
for high-performance
generative AI,

00:06:09.420 --> 00:06:11.430
then the hardware
is pre-validated

00:06:11.430 --> 00:06:14.430
so customers can deploy
with a confidence

00:06:14.430 --> 00:06:19.830
and without lengthy
qualification cycles.

00:06:19.830 --> 00:06:22.980
Now, actually, everything
when we talk in the context

00:06:22.980 --> 00:06:26.160
of AI runs on Kubernetes
native operation.

00:06:26.160 --> 00:06:28.740
The platform runs
Arc-enabled Kubernetes,

00:06:28.740 --> 00:06:31.380
which means AI workloads
are deployed and scaled

00:06:31.380 --> 00:06:36.300
and managed using the same
declarative operator-based

00:06:36.300 --> 00:06:40.560
approach that IT
teams already know

00:06:40.560 --> 00:06:44.310
for their containerized
application.

00:06:44.310 --> 00:06:47.730
And Foundry Local has been
installed as an Arc extension.

00:06:47.730 --> 00:06:51.690
No custom orchestration
or a proprietary tooling.

00:06:51.690 --> 00:06:53.340
If your team knows Kubernetes,

00:06:53.340 --> 00:06:56.400
they already know how
to operate this one.

00:06:56.400 --> 00:06:58.680
I called out the connected
and disconnected.

00:06:58.680 --> 00:06:59.610
It's very important.

00:06:59.610 --> 00:07:04.980
Azure Local is designed for the
full spectrum of connectivity.

00:07:04.980 --> 00:07:07.440
In connected mode, it
syncs model catalog

00:07:07.440 --> 00:07:11.100
and management policies from
the cloud through Azure Arc.

00:07:11.100 --> 00:07:14.040
And in fully disconnected
or air-gapped environment,

00:07:14.040 --> 00:07:16.020
the same infrastructure
continues

00:07:16.020 --> 00:07:17.280
to operate autonomously.

00:07:17.280 --> 00:07:20.550
It means that models are cached
locally and inference runs

00:07:20.550 --> 00:07:23.310
without any cloud dependency.

00:07:23.310 --> 00:07:28.590
And the operation teams actually
retain the full control.

00:07:28.590 --> 00:07:31.140
Of course, it comes with a
security and governance.

00:07:31.140 --> 00:07:33.990
So identity is handled
through Microsoft Entra ID

00:07:33.990 --> 00:07:39.720
with the JWT validation and
inference endpoints are secured

00:07:39.720 --> 00:07:43.680
with a TLS and API key or
token-based authentication.

00:07:43.680 --> 00:07:46.290
This is the same identity
and governance layer

00:07:46.290 --> 00:07:50.640
that customers use across
their cloud estate.

00:07:50.640 --> 00:07:53.760
But we're just extended
it to on-premises AI

00:07:53.760 --> 00:07:56.100
without any compromises.

00:07:56.100 --> 00:07:58.950
So Azure Local is not
just about the hardware,

00:07:58.950 --> 00:08:00.600
not just about the
infrastructure.

00:08:00.600 --> 00:08:05.160
It's the AI ready platform that
brings the Foundry capabilities

00:08:05.160 --> 00:08:07.590
from the cloud to
the enterprise edge

00:08:07.590 --> 00:08:09.300
with a pre-validated hardware,

00:08:09.300 --> 00:08:11.610
with a Kubernetes
native operation,

00:08:11.610 --> 00:08:16.680
as well as the security
that I was calling out.

00:08:16.680 --> 00:08:18.270
Now, I mentioned Foundry.

00:08:18.270 --> 00:08:19.710
So everybody knows

00:08:19.710 --> 00:08:24.210
that Microsoft has the
out-of-the-box offering

00:08:24.210 --> 00:08:27.570
to build agent on cloud,
on our public cloud.

00:08:27.570 --> 00:08:29.670
That's the Microsoft Foundry.

00:08:29.670 --> 00:08:32.760
And on the right
side of the screen,

00:08:32.760 --> 00:08:37.110
we have our on-device
inferencing offering.

00:08:37.110 --> 00:08:42.120
It's an SDK that optimized for
Windows and Mac OS and Android.

00:08:42.120 --> 00:08:45.000
Now, what we're introducing
today and announcing

00:08:45.000 --> 00:08:48.540
in a public preview is
Foundry Local that runs

00:08:48.540 --> 00:08:51.330
on the infrastructure
that I mentioned before,

00:08:51.330 --> 00:08:53.700
on Foundry Local
for both connected

00:08:53.700 --> 00:08:57.480
and disconnected scenarios for
single-node and multi-node

00:08:57.480 --> 00:09:01.950
across the different form
factors of Azure Local.

00:09:01.950 --> 00:09:06.660
Now, today, we're happy to
announce three capabilities

00:09:06.660 --> 00:09:09.210
when we say Foundry Local.

00:09:09.210 --> 00:09:11.550
On February this year,

00:09:11.550 --> 00:09:14.340
we've already announced
the availability

00:09:14.340 --> 00:09:18.480
of Foundry Local model catalog
on a single-node deployment.

00:09:18.480 --> 00:09:21.420
That was good for
those customers

00:09:21.420 --> 00:09:24.630
that has ONNX inferencing needs

00:09:24.630 --> 00:09:26.790
and single node is
sufficient for them.

00:09:26.790 --> 00:09:29.490
But those that really
needs to scale and run

00:09:29.490 --> 00:09:32.340
across multi-node
now can benefit

00:09:32.340 --> 00:09:35.580
from Foundry Local
model catalog,

00:09:35.580 --> 00:09:38.190
as well as inferencing
capabilities.

00:09:38.190 --> 00:09:40.260
So that is one of
our announcements.

00:09:40.260 --> 00:09:42.660
The second one is
around knowledge.

00:09:42.660 --> 00:09:46.680
We're happy to refresh
our RAG offering.

00:09:46.680 --> 00:09:49.920
That's a local RAG
offering that helps you

00:09:49.920 --> 00:09:52.980
to manage your knowledge
in the organization,

00:09:52.980 --> 00:09:56.340
and we will show you how it's
going to look like in a minute.

00:09:56.340 --> 00:09:57.720
As well as some tools.

00:09:57.720 --> 00:09:59.640
Now what tools means.

00:09:59.640 --> 00:10:05.490
It means that if you have a
custom MCP, that you would

00:10:05.490 --> 00:10:10.470
like to connect it to your local
sources, then now it's possible

00:10:10.470 --> 00:10:16.260
to do the mixture of choosing
the right model using RAG

00:10:16.260 --> 00:10:20.430
capabilities and connect
it through custom MCPs,

00:10:20.430 --> 00:10:23.520
and also other local tools
that you might have.

00:10:23.520 --> 00:10:25.770
So the combination
can come together,

00:10:25.770 --> 00:10:29.250
run on an Arc-enabled
Kubernetes environment

00:10:29.250 --> 00:10:33.480
across the different form
factors of Azure Local,

00:10:33.480 --> 00:10:36.870
from single-node to
multi-node deployment.

00:10:36.870 --> 00:10:39.330
Which scenarios do
we enable by that?

00:10:39.330 --> 00:10:41.610
So let's start to
look at the models.

00:10:41.610 --> 00:10:44.370
From left to right, you see
that there is now option

00:10:44.370 --> 00:10:47.130
to discover and deploy models.

00:10:47.130 --> 00:10:48.570
There is a catalog of models

00:10:48.570 --> 00:10:50.490
which are curated
for those scenarios.

00:10:50.490 --> 00:10:52.980
And there is also an option
to bring your own model.

00:10:52.980 --> 00:10:58.680
So if you have a container
with models that you choose

00:10:58.680 --> 00:11:01.050
from Hugging Face, let
me pick an example.

00:11:01.050 --> 00:11:04.440
Let's say YOLO 10 or any
predictive or generative AI,

00:11:04.440 --> 00:11:08.370
you can package this together
and mix it, and merge it

00:11:08.370 --> 00:11:12.210
with your own OCI registry.

00:11:12.210 --> 00:11:16.260
And we provide you the option to
serve these models, whether it's

00:11:16.260 --> 00:11:19.770
with a vLLM or with
an ONNX Runtime.

00:11:19.770 --> 00:11:23.340
On the agent and tool size, we
have now the option to connect

00:11:23.340 --> 00:11:27.240
to your local data with
those MCPs that I mentioned.

00:11:27.240 --> 00:11:30.630
We bring you a
reference application

00:11:30.630 --> 00:11:34.860
so you can build your local
chat experience, and of course,

00:11:34.860 --> 00:11:39.330
it comes with an option
also to build agents

00:11:39.330 --> 00:11:41.550
that can run locally and connect

00:11:41.550 --> 00:11:44.790
with that entire
platform offering.

00:11:44.790 --> 00:11:50.100
So today, we are announcing
Foundry Local model offering.

00:11:50.100 --> 00:11:53.430
It means that we are
expanding Foundry Local

00:11:53.430 --> 00:11:58.290
to include more community as
well as proprietary models.

00:11:58.290 --> 00:12:03.750
And all these are validated
and run on Azure Local

00:12:03.750 --> 00:12:06.420
for both connected and
disconnected scenarios.

00:12:06.420 --> 00:12:09.330
Now, on the model
platform offering,

00:12:09.330 --> 00:12:12.030
this is where the entire
stack is being managed

00:12:12.030 --> 00:12:13.560
by the customers.

00:12:13.560 --> 00:12:17.370
This is where we bring the
complete Foundry Local community

00:12:17.370 --> 00:12:20.730
models, the open source
models, and making sure

00:12:20.730 --> 00:12:22.260
that these models can run

00:12:22.260 --> 00:12:26.430
through the different form
factors or sizes of Azure Local

00:12:26.430 --> 00:12:29.940
from a single-node deployment
to multi-node deployment.

00:12:29.940 --> 00:12:34.200
And it comes with a pre-built
inferencing capabilities,

00:12:34.200 --> 00:12:38.370
like ONNX Runtime for single
node deployment and vLLM

00:12:38.370 --> 00:12:40.740
for multi-node deployment.

00:12:40.740 --> 00:12:43.650
And this one works
for both connected

00:12:43.650 --> 00:12:46.350
and fully disconnected
scenarios.

00:12:46.350 --> 00:12:54.180
The other option where we bring
the customers the different

00:12:54.180 --> 00:12:59.070
Foundry Local model called
model as a service.

00:12:59.070 --> 00:13:02.010
This is Microsoft-managed
offering,

00:13:02.010 --> 00:13:04.800
but that's the approach
for the customers really

00:13:04.800 --> 00:13:08.010
to get an access to
their frontier models.

00:13:08.010 --> 00:13:10.950
We are working with
Microsoft partners

00:13:10.950 --> 00:13:13.530
to bring the
proprietary IP models,

00:13:13.530 --> 00:13:18.630
so think about the Mistral and
OpenAI and others, to customers

00:13:18.630 --> 00:13:20.610
with the most
sensitive workloads

00:13:20.610 --> 00:13:24.480
where they cannot
access the cloud.

00:13:24.480 --> 00:13:27.660
We're still partnering with
some of these vendors,

00:13:27.660 --> 00:13:31.110
and there are specific
eligibility criteria,

00:13:31.110 --> 00:13:33.510
so it's not available
for everybody.

00:13:33.510 --> 00:13:36.750
Our customers are having
some geopolitical

00:13:36.750 --> 00:13:39.510
and sovereign-driven
model restrictions.

00:13:39.510 --> 00:13:43.350
So, for that purpose, we're
shipping also the option

00:13:43.350 --> 00:13:47.790
of model as a service offering.

00:13:47.790 --> 00:13:53.760
This is driven, for example,
by requesting EU only LLMs

00:13:53.760 --> 00:13:58.380
or geopolitical tension
or regulatory pressure.

00:13:58.380 --> 00:14:02.430
Some customers has concern
around foreign jurisdiction

00:14:02.430 --> 00:14:08.880
and external access and they
prefer a long-term strategic

00:14:08.880 --> 00:14:11.070
autonomy, fully disconnected.

00:14:11.070 --> 00:14:13.830
So this is the purpose
of having a model

00:14:13.830 --> 00:14:18.730
as a service option
for those customers.

00:14:19.830 --> 00:14:22.440
And both are valid.

00:14:22.440 --> 00:14:24.780
It means that -- at the
end, there is a link

00:14:24.780 --> 00:14:28.650
if you're interested either in
model as a platform or model

00:14:28.650 --> 00:14:33.030
as a service, just fill in the
form, and you can get access

00:14:33.030 --> 00:14:36.960
and support from
our product groups.

00:14:36.960 --> 00:14:41.040
Now, you see here how things
are being structured together.

00:14:41.040 --> 00:14:43.530
So on the infra layer,
we have Azure Local.

00:14:43.530 --> 00:14:46.260
On top of it, we have
the Kubernetes clusters.

00:14:46.260 --> 00:14:50.190
Then there is the inference
model, which could be ONNX

00:14:50.190 --> 00:14:53.160
for single node, vLLM, what
we are announcing today,

00:14:53.160 --> 00:14:54.600
for multi-node deployment.

00:14:54.600 --> 00:14:56.790
And on top of it, we have
some community model.

00:14:56.790 --> 00:14:58.560
You see here, just
a partial list.

00:14:58.560 --> 00:15:03.780
We have 71 different
models in the catalog.

00:15:03.780 --> 00:15:06.600
But a customer would need
to choose if the approach

00:15:06.600 --> 00:15:10.530
to manage these models is
through the platform itself

00:15:10.530 --> 00:15:13.620
or through the model as a
service that allows also

00:15:13.620 --> 00:15:16.710
to get access to
those frontier models

00:15:16.710 --> 00:15:19.170
that meets the
eligibility criteria

00:15:19.170 --> 00:15:21.030
that I mentioned before.

00:15:21.030 --> 00:15:25.410
So, in order to do that,
you see here, a screenshot

00:15:25.410 --> 00:15:32.190
from Azure Local with the option
now to get Foundry Local,

00:15:32.190 --> 00:15:34.350
everything that I've
described so far,

00:15:34.350 --> 00:15:37.140
so that the IT can now
decide to deploy this

00:15:37.140 --> 00:15:43.440
and make it available
for the developers.

00:15:43.440 --> 00:15:48.390
With that model offering that we
are proposing today in preview,

00:15:48.390 --> 00:15:53.070
I'm explicitly calling out
the inference layer support,

00:15:53.070 --> 00:15:56.460
which is generative
AI via ONNX Runtime

00:15:56.460 --> 00:15:58.500
for single-node deployment,

00:15:58.500 --> 00:16:02.880
plus vLLM for multi-node
high-performance serving,

00:16:02.880 --> 00:16:06.300
plus predictive AI workloads
through ONNX Runtime.

00:16:06.300 --> 00:16:09.660
So all models, whether it's
from the Foundry catalog,

00:16:09.660 --> 00:16:11.250
from the partner providers,

00:16:11.250 --> 00:16:14.130
or from the customer own
bring your own model,

00:16:14.130 --> 00:16:17.100
can run through one
consistent runtime

00:16:17.100 --> 00:16:21.640
with an OpenAI-compatible
REST endpoints.

00:16:22.920 --> 00:16:27.480
Key part of what we're actually
enabling today is the breadth

00:16:27.480 --> 00:16:29.220
of model catalog.

00:16:29.220 --> 00:16:34.140
Customers are not locked into
a single model strategy.

00:16:34.140 --> 00:16:37.320
They can choose across
proprietary, open weight,

00:16:37.320 --> 00:16:40.950
and specialized models
depending on the use case

00:16:40.950 --> 00:16:44.820
and the performance need and
the regulation constraints.

00:16:44.820 --> 00:16:49.720
It means that using frontier
models for reasoning

00:16:51.510 --> 00:16:54.600
or smaller efficient
models for edge

00:16:54.600 --> 00:16:57.990
and disconnected environments
is now possible.

00:16:57.990 --> 00:17:00.510
Customer can benefit from
a domain-tuned model

00:17:00.510 --> 00:17:04.560
for specific workloads and
all within the same platform.

00:17:04.560 --> 00:17:08.370
It results with flexibility
without compromises

00:17:08.370 --> 00:17:11.220
on the right model,
on the right place

00:17:11.220 --> 00:17:15.300
under full customer control.

